dotnet_build_calls": 0, "edit_calls": 0, "ended_on_rejection": false, "error": null, "exec_mode": "local", "failure_class": "solved", "failure_reason": null, "faults_served": 0, "final_answer_complete": true, "final_answer_present": true, "final_answer_source": "json", "final_workspace": "/workspace/openbench-ab/results/omp-screen5-b2/omp-19/omp-19/cells/without/trig-omp-multistep/5/workspace", "fresh_subagents": 0, "harness": "omp", "harness_version": "omp/12.18.3 (/workspace/pi-check/cache5/bin/omp/6e66a13a8fa112148a94c97b17706eed8dc1d73b/omp)", "harness_version_source": "host", "host": "grok-bot-vm-412615941", "image_digest": null, "last_activity_age_s": null, "list_has_generated": null, "main_agent_searched": false, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": 1000000, "model_max_tokens": 128000, "output_tail": "Fixed. The bug was in `ledger/currency.py`: `to_cents` used `int(amount * 100)`, which cuts off the fractional part instead of rounding. Floats are inexact (`19.99 * 100` is `1998.9999\u2026`), so `19.99` became `1998` cents and `0.29` became `28`. Because every total starts from `Line.cents()`, the subtotal, tax and discount results were all wrong too.\n\nThe fix changes `to_cents` to read the float as written in decimal (`Decimal(str(amount))`), multiply by 100, and round half up to whole cents. So `19.99` becomes `1999`, `0.29` becomes `29`, and `1.005` becomes `101`. Rounding halves away from zero also applies to negatives (`-2.675` becomes `-268`). Integers and numeric strings work too. Nothing else changed; the tax and discount code already rounds correctly in integer cents.\n\npytest isn't installed here, so I ran each test function directly with `python3`. All four tests in `tests/test_totals.py` and `test_format` in `tests/test_report.py` pass. I also checked the edge values above by hand.", "paced_wait_s": 0.0, "permission_rejections": 0, "read_calls": 0, "replies_ok": 4, "replies_throttled": 0, "requests_cache_read": 52094, "requests_ca